Z||000003||Raydar||12-10-2001||09:54 AM||||The rear cradle can be installed with Held Motorsports adapters. Coil-overs are required. There's not enough room in the earlier spaceframes for the stock 88 springs.<BR>The wiring? Good luck. I understand it's *all* different, and the bulkhead connectors are even in the wrong place.<BR>The front suspension? Well... One person on this forum has swapped an 88 front suspension into an earlier car, but it required a bunch of cutting and welding. Definitely not a bolt in. <BR>Better to find a tired 88 coupe and make it into a Formula. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>From the Department of Redundancy Department.</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Earl||12-10-2001||11:36 AM||||   can I use the 84 struts or just the springs with the 88 suspension? I have a 84 in good shape and very little money .(Thank God I worked as a mechanic for 6 years and a welder for 15)or I couldn't afford to own a fiero .<BR>   Can anyone tell me the dimensional differences from the 88 and 84 cradle .I would like to go with the v6 , better suspension,and better brakes of the 88 .<BR>   Am I opening a big can of worms hear ?<BR>I think I can get the 88 for about $300 or so?<BR>  opinions please<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000005||Raydar||12-10-2001||11:42 AM||||The issue is the angle of the strut/spring assembly. On the 88, they lean in toward the center of the car, more. On the earlier cars, they are more upright. The struts are very similar. Some vendors even make replacements that (theoretically) fit all years. If you put the 88 cradle in the older car, the coils will interfere with the inside of the shock towers. <P>Check here, and you'll know as much as I do about the swap...<BR> <A HREF="http://www.heldmotorsports.com" TARGET=_blank>www.heldmotorsports.com</A> <P>or  <A HREF="http://www.heldmotorsports.com/88cradle.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.heldmotorsports.com/88cradle.htm</A>  <P>They are *not* inexpensive, though.  <P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>From the Department of Redundancy Department.</FONT><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 12-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Rodrv6||12-10-2001||03:47 PM||||Unfortunatly, you've picked the two most different Fiero's possible  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>I had an 84 for many years and now have an 88.  While nothing is impossible, the amount of work needed to update the 84 to 88 specs is tremendous.  As has been mentioned, the front suspension is by no means a bolt in swap.  There are MAJOR differences in the basic chassis stampings in the front.  The rear is easier, but still takes special parts.  The 84 is also the most difficult to put a V-6 into because the rear and main wire harnesses are unique to the 84's especially the location of the rear main junction connecter that will be right where the forward exhaust manifold on the V-6 needs to be.<BR>There are a number of detail differences between the 84's and 88's.  For example, the routing of the coolant pipes where they cross the front crossmember is very different.  I don't want to come across as discouraging this sort of thing, but you'd probably be better off in the long run to find an 88 to start with and play from there.  But if you're into major projects----go for it!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  <BR>White 88 GT :)||||reg||1
Z||000007||Earl||12-10-2001||07:46 PM||||   Well I think Ill just use the front and rear bumper cover and the cradle . If I use the v6 Ill go with a "big" cam and carb.<P>  ||||reg||1
